          "quote": "+5V ... U6.6(SENSE,input)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "LTC4412 + P-ch MOSFET ... need reverse current protection mostly"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The reversed source/drain orientation undermines the LTC4412's intended ideal-diode control and can allow reverse current from the +5V rail toward USB VBUS, contrary to the documented protection goal.",
      "confidence": 0.94
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["SW6", "R34", "R35"],
      "pins": ["SW6.1", "SW6.2", "SW6.3", "SW6.4", "R34.1", "R35.1"],
      "nets": ["/CAN0_N", "Net-(R34-Pad2)", "Net-(R35-Pad2)", "/CAN1_N", "/CAN0_P", "/CAN1_P"],
      "observed": "The DIP-switch pins connect R34's CAN0_N termination leg to SW6.1, R35's CAN1_N leg to SW6.2, CAN1_P to SW6.3, and CAN0_P to SW6.4. With the standard two-switch pin pairing (1–2 and 3–4), closing the switches links the two negative legs through the resistors and directly joins the two positive CAN nets.",
      "expected": "The two CAN channels should remain separate; any termination/configuration switches should terminate each channel independently rather than bridge CAN0 and CAN1.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "/CAN0_N ... R34.1(1,passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "/CAN1_N ... R35.1(1,passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "/CAN0_P ... SW6.4(passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "/CAN1_P ... SW6.3(passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "Net-(R34-Pad2) ... R34.2(2,passive) SW6.1(passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "Net-(R35-Pad2) ... R35.2(2,passive) SW6.2(passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "Closing the DIP switches can couple the nominally separate CAN channels, defeating their separation and potentially causing bus interference or communication faults.",
      "confidence": 0.91
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The files show a reversed P-channel MOSFET in the LTC4412 USB power path, conflicting with the stated reverse-current-protection goal. They also wire the CAN DIP switch so that closing it bridges the two otherwise separate CAN channels."
}

